    "product_id": "628f3ee4-6af0-4aec-9367-75da9c9f2b96",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 010943\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n143 AM PST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Widespread wetting rain continues through the afternoon. Locally \n  heavy rainfall and a few instances of flash flooding possible. \n\n* Conditions dry out on Monday as gusty north winds pick up across \n  the region. Temperatures remain slightly below-normal next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nAfter isolated stronger storms passed through the region last \nevening, the night brings widespread stratiform rain. Most rainfall \nrates look to stay light to moderate but there will be potential for \nlocally increased rain rates. Some of these increased rain rates \nlook to be focused on higher peaks as well as in portions of Mohave, \nLincoln, eastern Clark, and far eastern San Bernardino counties. The \ntrend of persistent rain will lead to some flood risk, but the \nmomentary increased rain rates over these areas will cause concern \nfor flash flooding. Into the mid morning, higher resolution models \nshow breaks in the rain but still offer scattered showers into the \nafternoon. Breezy to gusty winds will build as we begin to see some \nof those breaks in rain. Shower activity begins to erode into the \nlate afternoon and early evening with winds decreasing into the \nnight. The last line of showers could offer spotty storm chances \nbefore they exit into that night.\n\nRainfall totals will favor the more eastern areas with higher \nelevations in Mohave County looking to break 2 inches of rainfall \novernight. The rest of Mohave County and most of Lincoln County will \nrange between 0.75 and 1.50\", the Colorado River Valley looks to \nrange from 0.5 to 1.00\", Las Vegas will be 0.25 to 0.50\", and the \nrest of the region will be up to 0.50\".\n\nThe rest of the forecast looks calm and cool with some potential for \nfor windy conditions next weekend.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package... \nWidespread rain is moving into the region with mostly light to \nmoderate rain rates expected. CIGs to fall to 6000 ft AGL then to \n4000 ft by the late night with moments of MVFR-level CIGs possible \ninto the early morning. CIGs begin to improve into the mid morning, \nas showers become become more spotty and breezy southwest winds \nbuild. Gusts increase to around 25 kts by Sunday afternoon but winds \nlook to ease into that night as the last of the showers exit.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Showers are becoming \nwidespread and will last through the night. Some waves of showers \ninto the early to mid morning will offer moments of lowered CIGS \n(around 2000 ft AGL) possible. Breezy to gust west to southwest \nwinds arrive into the mid to late morning as rain activity becomes \nmore spotty. Showers begin to erode from the west to east Sunday \nafternoon with winds beginning to reduce for most areas as showers \nexit. KDAG and KBIH look to remain breezy and gusty into Sunday \nnight.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/AVIATION...Murdock\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
